    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d316-a1">

                <pb facs="0356" n="296" sameAs="frus:frus1895p1/pg296"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 409.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Eustis to Mr.
                            Waller</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1895-11-18"><hi rendition="#italic">November 18,
                        1895</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sir</hi>: I believe your pardon can be obtained.
                    Its acceptance involves the admission of the validity of your trial, and would
                    bar your right to any claim on that account. Let me know whether pardon under
                    such terms is acceptable to yon. Release might be obtained within a very short
                    delay. I make this inquiry by instruction of my Government.</p>
                <p>Reply by telegraph if you can.</p>
                <p>Respectfully, yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. B. Eustis</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
